    "image_caption": null,
    "content_html": "<div class=\"articlebodycontent col-xl-9 col-lg-12 col-md-12 col-sm-12 col-12\" id=\"content-body-71653805\"><div class=\"schemaDiv\" id=\"schemaDiv\"><p><span class=\"drop-caps\">I</span> watched Moneyball again this week for the nth time, close to 15 years since its release. But this time, it felt different. It’s interesting how films have a way of telling you your own story without one even realising it.</p><p>In the ending scene, Brad drives home after getting the offer to be the Red Sox’s general manager. On the way, he plays a CD his daughter cut him. A thin, innocent voice starts singing, “I’m just a little bit caught in the middle, life’s a maze and love’s a riddle.” The camera zooms out to show a busy port; cranes big enough to make one’s life feel small. It zooms back in — on a misty-eyed Brad thinking about all the struggles he went through to get to this exact moment, and maybe, at this exact moment — realising what matters more. Brilliant. These movies aren’t merely entertainment. They are creative adaptations of real life, and that’s precisely what makes them live beyond their time.</p></div><div class=\"articleblock-container\" id=\"arthardpv\"></div><div class=\"update-publish-time article-published-time-end\"><p class=\"publish-time-new\"> Published <span>- November 08, 2026 03:50 am IST</span></p></div><div class=\"comments-shares share-page flex print-hide\" data-artid=\"71653805\" data-title=\"Caught in the middle\" data-url=\"https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/open-page/caught-in-the-middle/article71653805.ece\"><div class=\"left\"><div class=\"share\"><button class=\"btn share-btn\" data-ga-labelclick=\"ArticleShare\"></button><ul class=\"share-list bottom\" style=\"display: none;\"><li class=\"copy-link\" data-cf-modified-bf9ed0137a747eed8c999480-=\"\" onclick=\"if (!window.__cfRLUnblockHandlers) return false; copytext('https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/open-page/caught-in-the-middle/article71653805.ece');\">\n               