    "content_text": "I\nwatched Moneyball again this week for the nth time, close to 15 years since its release. But this time, it felt different. It’s interesting how films have a way of telling you your own story without one even realising it.\nIn the ending scene, Brad drives home after getting the offer to be the Red Sox’s general manager. On the way, he plays a CD his daughter cut him. A thin, innocent voice starts singing, “I’m just a little bit caught in the middle, life’s a maze and love’s a riddle.” The camera zooms out to show a busy port; cranes big enough to make one’s life feel small. It zooms back in — on a misty-eyed Brad thinking about all the struggles he went through to get to this exact moment, and maybe, at this exact moment — realising what matters more. Brilliant. These movies aren’t merely entertainment. They are creative adaptations of real life, and that’s precisely what makes them live beyond their time.\nPublished\n- November 08, 2026 03:50 am IST\nCopy link\nEmail\nFacebook\nTwitter\nTelegram\nLinkedIn\nWhatsApp\nReddit\nREAD LATER\nSEE ALL\nRemove",
